[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the entire BGP leaf–spine–super-spine fabric and found no evidence of bouncing neighbors or unstable links. All FRR daemons are active. On every router, BGP sessions are Established with “Connections established 1; dropped 0” and consistent uptime (~2 minutes) across all peers. Examples: spine_router_0_0 peers 172.16.0.0, .5, .7 are Established; spine_router_0_1 peers 172.16.0.2, .9, .11 are Established; both leaves are Established to their spines; the super-spine is Established to both spines. Routing tables are correct end-to-end, advertising 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 as expected. 

Interfaces eth0/eth1/eth2 on all routers show link detected: yes with no errors. Kernel logs only show tap interface renames during lab bring-up, not link up/down events. FRR logs on all nodes show a brief flurry of messages during initial session establishment (e.g., “nexthop_set failed … resetting connection”) followed immediately by stable keepalives and End-of-RIB—no ongoing resets. Host reachability pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 shows 0% loss on quick tests; longer “50 ping” and iperf attempts hit the tool’s 10s timeout, not a network issue.

Affected component: none; the fabric control plane and data plane are operating normally.
Mechanism: If sessions were bouncing, FRR would show resets or incremented drop counters, which it does not.
Localization: All routers’ BGP adjacencies and interfaces are stable.
Root cause: No network fault found; earlier transient logs align with initial boot only.
Corrective fix: No changes required on the fabric. If application “session drops” persist, investigate endpoint/app causes and capture packets; optionally enable BFD for faster fault detection and add neighbor-change alerts for monitoring. 
=== END ===